<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

  M8.5 review finding F1. The app targets SDK 36 (`app/build.gradle.kts`), and
  Android's default cleartext policy for apps targeting API 28+ refuses
  cleartext traffic everywhere, including loopback. The platform only grew an
  implicit localhost exception at API 37 — see "Localhost configuration" at
  https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/security-config, checked
  2026-09-22 — so on every API level this app actually ships to below that
  (26 through 36), a plain `HttpURLConnection` GET to `127.0.0.1` is refused
  by the platform, not by anything in this app's own code.

  MetricsClient.fetchMetricsPayload needs exactly that GET, to read xray's
  `/debug/vars` for the per-tag traffic breakdown (ARCHITECTURE.md §14.4).
  Without this file, the feature is silently dead on every shipped OS version
  except the one the original device verification happened to run on.

  This is deliberately the narrowest exception the platform allows:
  - Only the literal loopback address `127.0.0.1`, not the `localhost` domain
    name — `MetricsClient` never resolves a hostname, it dials the literal
    address directly, so the domain name form would be broader than what is
    actually used.
  - `includeSubdomains="false"` — there is nothing under `127.0.0.1` to
    include, and leaving this explicit rather than omitted (its default is
    also false) states the intent instead of relying on a default a future
    reader would have to go look up.
  - No `<base-config>` override: every other destination keeps the platform
    default (cleartext refused). Do NOT replace this with
    `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"` on the <application> tag — that is a
    global relaxation, and loopback is not "the internet" this app talks to.

  Applies to ALL build types, not wrapped in <debug-overrides>. The metrics
  listener this exception exists for is opt-in (ARCHITECTURE.md §14.4) but
  not debug-only: a user can enable the per-tag breakdown in a release build,
  and the listener that switch turns on runs in release the same as in debug.
  Restricting this file to debug would leave the release build exactly as
  broken as before this fix for the same population that can turn the switch
  on.

  Before deleting this file because minSdk has risen past 36: re-read
  "Localhost configuration" in the URL above and confirm the floor is at or
  above API 37 first. Below that, this exception is still load-bearing.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
